WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.482
Estimate a rental as a complete session.

00:00:03.482 --> 00:00:09.831
Include provisioning, package installation, model transfer, data preparation, active computation, evaluation, troubleshooting, and cleanup.

00:00:09.831 --> 00:00:17.410
Every running minute may be billed at the full accelerator rate, even when the GPU is idle.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:28.265
Multiply the rate by realistic elapsed time rather than ideal compute time, then include CPU, memory, and operating-system surcharges if the provider prices them separately.

00:00:28.265 --> 00:00:30.928
Storage has an independent lifecycle.

00:00:30.928 --> 00:00:38.506
Persistent disks, snapshots, model caches, datasets, logs, and object storage can remain billable after an instance stops.

00:00:38.506 --> 00:00:44.446
Data transfer may be charged when files cross regions or leave the provider.

00:00:44.446 --> 00:00:53.253
Place compute near required data, avoid copying artifacts unnecessarily, and understand whether stopping an instance retains its disks and addresses.

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:57.144
Use on-demand rental for uncertain or short work.

00:00:57.144 --> 00:01:03.084
Consider reserved capacity only when utilization is predictable enough to justify the commitment.

00:01:03.084 --> 00:01:09.434
Use interruptible capacity for jobs that save checkpoints, resume safely, and tolerate delayed completion.

00:01:09.434 --> 00:01:18.241
A low spot price is not a saving if repeated interruption loses hours of work or corrupts an unprotected output.

00:01:18.241 --> 00:01:23.361
Set project budgets, spend alerts, quotas, and automatic expiration where available.

00:01:23.361 --> 00:01:26.843
Label resources with an owner and purpose.

00:01:26.843 --> 00:01:33.602
Schedule development shutdowns and use job wrappers that release compute after both success and failure.

00:01:33.602 --> 00:01:39.132
Alerts may notify rather than stop spending, so assign someone to respond.

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:45.482
At the end of each session, review compute, storage, snapshots, public addresses, and transfer.

00:01:45.482 --> 00:01:51.012
Shutdown discipline is an engineering control, not a reminder to be careful.
